<Base title="About & CV" active="About"><div class="shell page-body"><header class="page-heading"><p class="eyebrow">A little about me</p><h1>Curiosity across scales</h1><p class="lede">I’m Syver Døving Agdestein, a postdoctoral researcher in scientific computing at CWI in Amsterdam.</p></header><div class="about-intro"><div class="prose"><p>I work on probabilistic simulation of turbulence, closure models, and differentiable programming in Julia. The central question: how do we account for the detail that a practical simulation cannot resolve?</p><p>I did my PhD at Eindhoven University of Technology on data-driven closure models for large-eddy simulation, treating the numerical discretization as part of the physics.</p><p>Away from research, I’m drawn to history, languages, and the piano. I’ve lived in Asker, Gembloux, Toulouse, Saint Petersburg, Paris, and now Amsterdam.</p></div><img src="/logo.png" alt="Syver Døving Agdestein" width="220" height="220" /></div><section class="cv-section"><h2>Employment</h2><Timeline items={employment}/></section><section class="cv-section"><h2>Education</h2><Timeline items={education} kind="education"/></section><section class="cv-section"><h2>Teaching & supervision</h2><div class="prose"><p>Master’s thesis supervision:</p><ul><li>Lucas Ronckers — <em>Probabilistic turbulence modeling with ideal large eddy simulation: Bayesian inverse filtering and flow matching</em> — Eindhoven University of Technology (2026)</li><li>Viviane Desgrange — <em>An inverse problem approach for closure modelling</em> — University of Amsterdam (2023)</li></ul><p>Lectures at schools and masterclasses:</p><ul><li><em>Learning neural closure models for fluid flows</em> — Autumn School on Scientific Machine Learning, CWI (October 2023)</li><li><em>Learning physics from data</em> — Masterclass on Machine Learning for Inverse Problems: A Bayesian Perspective, CWI (May 2022)</li></ul></div></section><section class="cv-section"><h2>Service & outreach</h2><div class="prose"><ul><li>Peer review: Computer Methods in Applied Mechanics and Engineering (CMAME); Journal of Computational Physics</li><li>CWI works council (2023–2025)</li></ul></div></section><section class="cv-section"><h2>Skills & languages</h2><div class="prose"><p><strong>Research:</strong> Numerical analysis, partial differential equations, finite-element and finite-volume methods, spectral methods, turbulence and large-eddy simulation, structure-preserving machine learning, and generative modeling.</p><p><strong>Computing:</strong> Julia, Python, MATLAB, high-performance computing with Slurm, differentiable programming, multi-GPU kernel programming, Git, continuous integration, and agentic workflows.</p><p><strong>Languages:</strong> Fluent in Norwegian, English, and French; basic Russian and Dutch.</p></div></section></div></Base>
